// Raw-memory primitives and the page reserve the collector (`./gc.bit`, #1363)
// is built on. Sibling file, so the same module — no import needed.
//
// WHY A RESERVE EXISTS
// --------------------
// `runtime/alloc/alloc.bit` (#1362) is the real allocator and the collector
// calls it, exactly as `runtime/gc/` calls `runtime/alloc/`. But that port pushed the OS
// page fetch OUT to its caller on purpose: `syscall` is Linux-only and
// `extern function` Darwin-only, and both are rejected on the DECLARATION, so a
// module containing either cannot compile for the other target. The allocator's
// core is therefore platform-free and `heapRefillLocked` takes a span address
// rather than calling mmap.
//
// The collector inherits that constraint unchanged. Bit has no function
// pointers in the unmanaged subset, so a platform-free `gc.bit` cannot be
// handed a page-mapping CALLBACK — the only thing it can be handed is MEMORY.
// So the caller maps pages (binding `runtime/alloc/linux` or
// `runtime/alloc/darwin`, whichever its target wants) and hands the collector a
// contiguous reserve; the collector bumps spans out of it to refill size
// classes. That keeps `gc.bit` compiling for all three targets while the real
// size-class allocator does all the actual allocation policy.
//
// The reserve's OWN bump cursor is still forward-only: it never takes an
// individual span back into its bump space by rewinding. But a released span
// need not be lost: `gcHeapFree`'s overflow arm (the #3045 span cache is full)
// hands it to the SPAN FREE LIST below (`spanFreeListPush`/`spanFreeListTake`,
// #3868), which `gcHeapAlloc`'s refill path consults before growing the
// reserve. #2803 measured what happens without this: every span address lost
// past the cache burned a whole 4 MiB chunk that was then abandoned forever,
// ~9.5 chunks per LSP edit on a mid-sized project.
//
// A SPAN THE FREE LIST WON'T TAKE IS RELEASED TO THE OS ANYWAY (#2375), by
// `munmap` of that 128 KiB alone — today that is only the fallback inside
// `spanFreeListPush` itself, when the OS refuses the MAP_FIXED reset the push
// tries first (should not happen for a range this reserve already owns). The
// reserve's bump cursor does not rewind over an unmapped span, so in that one
// case the ADDRESS RANGE really is abandoned while the physical memory goes
// back — which is still the point on that path, since RSS is what ratchets
// (#2325).
//
// `reserveRelease` therefore unmaps chunks that already have holes in them
// (some spans truly unmapped, some merely decommitted-and-relinked by the free
// list), which is fine — munmap of a range containing unmapped OR decommitted
// pages is not an error — and a chunk's own two link words can never be inside
// a span, because the first span-aligned take out of a chunk starts strictly
// above `base + chunkHdrBytes`.
//
// WHOLE CHUNKS, HOWEVER, ARE KNOWABLE AND RELEASABLE (#2373). Until this ticket
// they were neither: `reserveGrow` mapped a chunk, rebased the descriptor onto
// it and discarded the previous chunk's address, so the pages could not be
// unmapped even in principle. The three-word descriptor now threads a list of
// the chunks IT mapped through the chunks themselves, and `reserveRelease`
// walks it. Two things follow, and only the second is a behaviour change:
// `gcDeinit` returns the collector's mapped memory to the OS, and a span is
// now aligned to `allocSpanBytes` so that a later ticket can reason about
// which span an address belongs to at all (#2374).
//
// WHAT THE RESERVE IS NOT: A CAP (#1638)
// --------------------------------------
// It was one, and that was a bug, not a design. `boot` mapped a single fixed
// 8 MiB arena, `reserveTake` returned 0 the moment the bump hit its end, and
// nothing anywhere mapped another page — so the heap's capacity was a compile
// time constant while `runtime/alloc/`'s is the machine's. Four 2 MiB slices was
// enough to hit it; the first succeeded and the rest silently came back null.
//
// The fix is not a larger constant — that only moves the cliff. `reserveGrow`
// below gives the required behaviour: when the reserve cannot satisfy a
// request, map a fresh chunk from the OS and bump out of that instead. The
// reserve becomes what its name says, a batching buffer over `mapPages`, and
// the boot arena becomes merely the first chunk.
//
// A DISCONTIGUOUS HEAP IS SAFE, and that is worth stating because a bump arena
// invites the opposite assumption. Nothing in the collector requires one span:
// `gcLoAddr`/`gcHiAddr` (../gc/gcalloc.bit) is a monotonic min/max REJECT
// window that is allowed to span holes, the address->object index is a hash
// table keyed on the exact body address, and `heapRefillLocked` already takes
// an arbitrary span address. Chunks are simply new spans in a different place.
//
// Everything here is `@nosplit` (§10.3): it is the memory the collector and
// allocator are built on, so it must not allocate through the managed heap and
// must not reach a safepoint.

// A reserve descriptor is three words, and THREE IS A FIXED WIDTH rather than a
// convenience: `runtime/root/{linux,darwin}/boot.bit` spells the channel
// descriptor as a literal `[3]i64`, and `../gc.bit` embeds two of them in a
// state block whose width `runtime/root/root.bit` refuses to boot unless it is
// exactly `gcWords` (39, #3292 grew it 37->38 to add `gcNextObjects`, #3868
// grew it 38->39 for the span free list head below). A fourth reserve word is
// not free; it is three edits in two other modules — same shape #3292 paid to
// grow `gcWords` itself. #3868 deliberately did NOT take that route: only the
// SPAN reserve (`gcSpanRes`) ever needs a free-list head, so `gc.bit` owns one
// dedicated word (`gcSpanFree`) instead of widening every reserve — the
// channel descriptor and the address-index reserve stay three words, untouched.
export const reserveWords = 3

// Head of the list of chunks THIS reserve mapped from the OS, 0 = none.
//
// It occupies the word the arena's BASE address used to, which nothing ever
// read: a bump allocator needs a cursor (`resBump`) and a limit (`resEnd`), and
// the base was only ever the cursor's initial value. Reusing it is what makes
// the chunk list fit in the fixed three words above.
//
// The list is threaded through the chunks themselves — `chunkNextOff` and
// `chunkLenOff` at each chunk's own base — so remembering a chunk costs no
// allocation. Memory a CALLER handed to `reserveInit` is never on this list;
// see `reserveRelease` for why that distinction is the whole point.
const resChunks = 0
const resEnd = 1
const resBump = 2

// Byte offsets of a mapped chunk's own two header words, written into the first
// 16 bytes of the chunk. The bump therefore starts at `base + chunkHdrBytes`.
const chunkNextOff = 0
const chunkLenOff = 8
const chunkHdrBytes = 16

// The `*i64` addressing the 8-aligned machine word at `addr`. `*i64` arithmetic
// scales by 8, so stepping that many elements off the null pointer lands
// exactly on `addr` (§11.4). This is the inverse of `int(p)` and the only way
// to turn an address back into a pointer — Bit has no pointer-to-pointer cast.
//
// `addr` MUST be 8-aligned. Every address the collector fabricates is: object
// bases come from the allocator (page-aligned spans carved into 8-multiple
// classes) and every offset added to one is a multiple of 8.
//
// PINNED (ABI.md §9). A sibling runtime object spells a cross-module call by the
// callee's emitted symbol, and an unpinned export is emitted as `m<N>$name`
// where N is assigned by the IMPORTING build — a name no separately emitted
// object can ever define. `runtime/root` calls these, so they must be pinned or
// its freestanding emit is refused (SPEC §11.9).
//
// The `bit_rt_port_` prefix is deliberate and PERMANENT: ABI.md names no
// `mem_word_at`/`mem_load_word`/`mem_store_word`/`mem_load_byte`/`mem_store_byte`
// symbol, so no rename ever touches these — they exist only so a cross-module
// callee resolves under `--freestanding` (SPEC §11.9).
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_mem_word_at") fn wordAt(addr: int): *i64 {
  let n: *i64 = nil
  return n + (addr / 8)
}

export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_mem_load_word") fn loadWord(addr: int): int {
  let p = wordAt(addr)
  let v = *p
  return v
}

export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_mem_store_word") fn storeWord(addr: int, v: int) {
  let p = wordAt(addr)
  *p = v
}

// A `*u8` addressing the byte at `addr`. `*u8` arithmetic scales by 1, so the
// offset IS the address. Used only for the header's 1-byte mark flag, which is
// written as a byte rather than a word so the header's bit pattern matches the
// fixed by ABI.md §1: `marked` is 1 byte at +24, followed by padding.
@nosplit fn byteAt(addr: int): *u8 {
  let n: *u8 = nil
  return n + addr
}

export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_mem_load_byte") fn loadByte(addr: int): int {
  let p = byteAt(addr)
  let v = *p
  return int(v)
}

export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_mem_store_byte") fn storeByte(addr: int, v: int) {
  let p = byteAt(addr)
  *p = u8(v)
}

// Round `n` up to the next multiple of `m`.
@nosplit fn memRoundUp(n: int, m: int): int {
  let r = n % m
  if (r == 0) {
    return n
  }
  return n + (m - r)
}

// Round `n` up to the next multiple of the POWER OF TWO `a`. The mask form, and
// the only alignment the reserve needs: the sole value passed for `a` here is
// `allocSpanBytes`.
//
// `allocSpanBytes` is `../alloc`'s and is in scope without an import of its
// own: an import binds the whole MODULE, and `./gc.bit` — this file's sibling,
// so the same module (§17.1) — already imports it. Importing it here as well is
// E0042, not a second binding.
@nosplit fn memAlignUp(n: int, a: int): int {
  return (n + a - 1) & ~(a - 1)
}

// Initialise a reserve over `[addr, addr + bytes)`. The caller owns the
// storage and must have obtained it from the OS (mapped pages) or from memory
// that is already resident for the process lifetime.
//
// PINNED (ABI.md §9): `runtime/chan`'s `chan_make` reserve is provisioned by
// this primitive from `boot` (via chan's `chanBootBind`), a cross-module call a
// freestanding chan emit spells by the callee's emitted symbol — an unpinned
// export is `m<N>$name`, a name no separately emitted object can define (#1613).
// Same `bit_rt_port_` placeholder as every other cross-module runtime helper.
// CALL IT ONCE PER RESERVE. It clears the chunk list, so calling it a second
// time on a reserve that has since grown forgets every chunk `reserveGrow`
// mapped — exactly the leak this ticket removed. `reserveGrow` therefore
// rebases the descriptor itself instead of calling this.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_mem_reserve_init") fn reserveInit(r: *i64, addr: int, bytes: int) {
  *(r + resChunks) = 0
  *(r + resEnd) = addr + bytes
  *(r + resBump) = addr
}

// Take `n` bytes from the reserve. Returns 0 when exhausted — the
// same "out of memory" sentinel the allocator uses, and the one `gcAlloc`
// propagates as `runtime/gc/`'s `?[*]u8` null.
//
// A TAKE OF AT LEAST `allocSpanBytes` IS ALIGNED TO `allocSpanBytes` (#2373);
// anything smaller stays 8-aligned. The rule is by SIZE rather than by caller
// because both matter:
//
//   - #2374 finds the span owning a slot by masking the slot's address with
//     `~(allocSpanBytes - 1)`, which is sound only if EVERY span starts on that
//     boundary. Enforced here it stays true for a second span consumer; passed
//     in by the one call site it stays true until someone forgets.
//   - Aligning the SMALL takes too would cost a whole span each, and the
//     channel reserve makes thousands of 128-byte ones (#1680). So a take large
//     enough to be a span is treated as one, and nothing else is.
//
// It is also stronger than page alignment on every target, deliberately: `boot`
// passes 4096 as the nominal page size even on aarch64-macos, where the real
// page is 16 KiB, so "align to the page size the collector was told about"
// would not have produced a page-aligned span there. 131072 is a multiple of
// both.
//

// PINNED (ABI.md §9): `runtime/chan`'s `chan_make` carves each channel handle
// and (buffered) ring out of the boot-provisioned reserve with this call, across
// the object boundary — so it needs the emitted-symbol contract every
// cross-module runtime call relies on (#1613). See `reserveInit`'s pin note.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_mem_reserve_take") fn reserveTake(r: *i64, n: int): int {
  if (n <= 0) {
    return 0
  }
  let want = memRoundUp(n, 8)
  let bump = *(r + resBump)
  let end = *(r + resEnd)
  let start = bump
  if (want >= allocSpanBytes) {
    start = memAlignUp(bump, allocSpanBytes)
  }
  if (start + want > end) {
    return 0
  }
  *(r + resBump) = start + want
  return start
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The OS page seam (#1638)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `runtime/gc` must compile for all three targets, so it cannot contain a
// `syscall` (Linux-only) or a libc `extern function` with a Darwin-only symbol
// — both are rejected on the DECLARATION, for the whole module, even uncalled
// (SPEC §11.7/§11.8). The page mapper therefore lives per-OS and this module
// reaches it as an ordinary undefined symbol resolved at link.
//
// That shape only became legal in the last two commits and both were needed:
// #1640 admitted `extern function` calls inside a `@nosplit` body (the whole
// allocator is `@nosplit`, so without it nothing on the allocation path could
// call this), and #1645 stopped `--emit-obj` rejecting an `extern` whose
// definition is not in an archive it never reads. Do not "simplify" this into
// a direct call to `mapPages`: importing either provider makes `runtime/gc`
// single-platform and fails 42 of the 63 freestanding emits.
//
// ONE DEFINITION ANSWERS TO THIS NAME: `runtime/root/{linux,darwin}`'s
// `rtMapPages`, pinned `bit_rt_map_pages`.
//
// The spelling matters and the obvious alternative is wrong. Naming this
// `bit_rt_port_alloc_map_pages` — the page provider's own port-internal pin —
// collides the moment a program compiles `runtime/alloc/darwin` from source AND
// links an archive that already defines the provider.
// `_tests_/stress/roothostdarwin` does exactly that, and is how the first attempt
// was caught (DuplicateSymbol). Keep the ABI name here.
extern fn bit_rt_map_pages(nbytes: i64): i64

// The release half of the same seam (#1641). `runtime/alloc/`'s `freeLarge` calls
// `unmapPages` directly; this module cannot, for exactly the reasons above, so
// it names a symbol and lets the link resolve it. Returns non-zero on success,
// and the result is ignored on purpose: a munmap of a range this module itself
// mapped cannot fail for any reason a caller could act on.
extern fn bit_rt_unmap_pages(addr: i64, nbytes: i64): i64

// The span free list's decommit primitive (#3868): an atomic MAP_FIXED reset
// of `[addr, addr+nbytes)` to a fresh, zeroed, private mapping AT THE SAME
// ADDRESS. The OS drops whatever physical pages backed the old mapping as
// part of installing the new one — a decommit — while the address stays
// valid, unlike `bit_rt_unmap_pages` — a recommit. Non-zero on success. See
// `spanFreeListPush` for why both properties are required together.
extern fn bit_rt_remap_pages(addr: i64, nbytes: i64): i64

// Heap exhaustion is FATAL, and this is the seam that makes it loud (#1642).
//
// `runtime/root/`'s allocating doors are uniformly `g_gc.alloc(info) orelse
// fatal("out of memory")` — the runtime must never return a null object to
// compiled code. This port used to, because `@nosplit` cannot call `panic`, so
// every door returned 0 and bumped a counter nothing reads; the observable
// result was three wrong answers and a null store rather than a diagnostic.
//
// An `extern function` call IS legal in a `@nosplit` body (#1640), so the door
// can reach `fatal` directly. NEVER RETURNS: it writes
// `panic: out of memory` to fd 2 and exits 2.
// The `i64` result exists only because Bit has no `noreturn` in the unmanaged
// subset; no caller may use it.
extern fn bit_rt_oom(): i64

// Bytes the reserve asks the OS for when it has to grow. Chosen the way
// `allocSpanBytes` is: large enough that growth is amortised to roughly one
// syscall per chunk rather than one per span, small enough that a program with
// a small live set does not reserve address space it will never touch.
const reserveChunkBytes = 4194304

// Take `n` bytes from the reserve, mapping a fresh chunk from the OS when it
// no longer fits. Returns 0 only when the OS itself refuses.
//
// This is the growth `reserveTake` deliberately does not have, kept separate so
// the plain bump take stays available to a caller that really does own a fixed
// arena.
//
// `runtime/chan`'s channel reserve USED to be cited here as such a caller, and
// that was the #1680 hang: `chan_make` carved every handle and ring out of one
// fixed 1 MiB arena, and on exhaustion returned 0 — a NIL CHANNEL, which
// `bit_rt_chan_send` parks on forever (SPEC §16.2). `_tests_/imports/http3e2e`
// reached it with ~12 KiB left and a 32 KiB `chan<StreamChunk>(4096)` to make.
// `runtime/chan/` allocates both from `page_allocator`, unbounded, so a fixed arena
// was never the oracle's behaviour. It grows through here now, exactly as the
// heap (`../gc.bit`) and the address index (`./gcindex.bit`) already do — which
// is the lesson this file's own header records: a larger constant only moves the
// cliff.
//
// Rebasing abandons whatever is left in the current chunk. That is bounded by
// the largest single request the reserve serves and happens at most once per
// chunk, so it is a rounding error against `reserveChunkBytes`.
//
// The abandoned chunk is REMEMBERED, though (#2373): it is pushed onto the
// reserve's chunk list, threaded through its own first two words, before the
// descriptor moves on. That is what `reserveRelease` walks, and it is why this
// function rebases the three words by hand rather than calling `reserveInit`,
// which clears the list.
//
// PINNED (ABI.md §9): `runtime/chan`'s `chan_make` calls it across the object
// boundary, so it needs the emitted-symbol contract rather than a per-build
// mangled name (#1613). See `reserveTake`'s pin note. `bit_rt_port_` is not an
// ABI name, so no rename (#1583) collides it with an RtFn target.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_mem_reserve_grow") fn reserveGrow(r: *i64, n: int): int {
  let addr = reserveTake(r, n)
  if (addr != 0) {
    return addr
  }
  if (n <= 0) {
    return 0
  }
  // A request larger than a chunk gets its own chunk, sized for the request
  // PLUS the two link words at the chunk's base and, for a take big enough to
  // be span-aligned, the gap ahead of the first aligned address. Without that
  // slack an exactly-`want`-sized chunk would not fit the take that asked for
  // it and this would return 0 with memory in hand.
  let want = memRoundUp(n, 8)
  let slack = chunkHdrBytes
  if (want >= allocSpanBytes) {
    slack = chunkHdrBytes + allocSpanBytes
  }
  let chunk = memRoundUp(want + slack, reserveChunkBytes)
  let base = int(bit_rt_map_pages(i64(chunk)))
  if (base == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  // Push before bumping: the link words are the chunk's own first 16 bytes, so
  // the new bump starts past them.
  storeWord(base + chunkNextOff, *(r + resChunks))
  storeWord(base + chunkLenOff, chunk)
  *(r + resChunks) = base
  *(r + resEnd) = base + chunk
  *(r + resBump) = base + chunkHdrBytes
  return reserveTake(r, n)
}

// Is `[addr, addr + nbytes)` inside the chunk THIS reserve most recently mapped
// from the OS? #2375's licence to `munmap` an individual span, and it must be
// asked of every span before one is released.
//
// The distinction is the same one `reserveRelease` draws and it matters for the
// same reason, one step finer: a caller may bind memory it does not want touched.
// `boot` binds an 8 MiB arena of its own, and five stress programs bind a
// Bit-MANAGED `[]i64` (`_tests_/stress/gcreserve` among them) — unmapping a span
// carved out of that punches a hole in the live heap. Only `reserveGrow` pushes
// onto the chunk list, so only what it mapped can answer true here.
//
// THE HEAD CHUNK IS THE ONLY ONE WORTH ASKING ABOUT, because the answer is only
// ever needed immediately after the take that produced `addr`, and the bump
// cursor is always inside the most recently mapped chunk (`reserveGrow` sets
// `resEnd` and `resChunks` together; `reserveInit` sets `resEnd` with an empty
// list). Checking containment explicitly rather than inferring it from
// `resChunks != 0` keeps that reasoning out of the load-bearing path: a caller
// that asks about some other address gets a truthful `false` instead of a
// licence to unmap it.
//
// STILL TRUE WITH THE SPAN FREE LIST (#3868) BELOW, and deliberately so: a
// free-list-recycled span never reaches this function at all. It is popped by
// `spanFreeListTake`, not by `reserveTake`/`reserveGrow`, so `gcHeapAlloc`
// never calls this on one — see that function's refill comment for why it is
// always `owned = true` by construction instead. Widening this check to scan
// every chunk (so it could also answer for an OLDER chunk's recycled span)
// would be needless: nothing ever asks it to.
export @nosplit fn reserveHeadOwns(r: *i64, addr: int, nbytes: int): bool {
  let c = *(r + resChunks)
  if (c == 0 || addr <= 0 || nbytes <= 0) {
    return false
  }
  let chunkLen = loadWord(c + chunkLenOff)
  return addr >= c && addr + nbytes <= c + chunkLen
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The span free list (#3868) — recovering a released span's ADDRESS, not
// just its pages
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `reserveGrow`'s bump never rewinds, so a span released past the #3045 span
// cache's 64 slots was previously lost forever: its pages went back via
// `memUnmapPages`, but its address did not, and the next refill had to map a
// fresh 4 MiB chunk. Measured on a churning `bit lsp --stdio`: ~9.5 abandoned
// chunks per edit on a 16-file project, each retaining a resident header page
// for the rest of the process's life (#2803, #3868).
//
// EVERY SPAN THIS LIST EVER HOLDS IS THE SAME SIZE — `reserveTake` aligns any
// take of at least `allocSpanBytes` to it (#2373) — so this needs no
// coalescing and no size search: a plain singly-linked stack, threaded
// through each span's own first word exactly as `reserveGrow`'s chunk list
// threads through each chunk's first two.
//
// THE TWO INVARIANTS THAT MAKE THIS SAFE, both from the ticket that added it:
//
//   1. OWNERSHIP. `reserveHeadOwns` still gates whether a span may become
//      unmapped/recycled at all — unchanged, see its own comment above. This
//      list is pushed to from exactly one place, `gcHeapFree`'s overflow arm,
//      and that arm only ever reaches a span `heapReclaimSpansLocked`
//      reclaimed — which only takes spans with `spanOwned != 0` to begin with
//      (see `gcHeapAlloc`'s refill comment in `../gc.bit`). So every span
//      here was already established as reserve-owned before it arrived, and
//      handing it back out needs no re-check.
//
//   2. THE PAGES STILL GO BACK TO THE OS — the #2375 retention leak is not
//      reintroduced. A span sitting mapped-and-idle in this list would BE
//      that leak with a different data structure wrapped around it. So
//      `spanFreeListPush` does not simply remember the address: it first
//      REPLACES the span's mapping with a fresh one at the SAME address
//      (`bit_rt_remap_pages`, an atomic MAP_FIXED reset). The OS drops
//      whatever physical pages backed the old mapping as part of installing
//      the new one, and the new one is untouched — 0 bytes resident — until
//      something writes to it. The single word this function writes next,
//      the "next" link, is the only byte that becomes resident again; the
//      rest of the span stays unbacked for as long as it sits in the list.
//
// THE HEAD LIVES OUTSIDE THE 3-WORD RESERVE DESCRIPTOR ON PURPOSE: only the
// SPAN reserve (`gcSpanRes`) ever needs one, and widening `reserveWords`
// would cost the channel reserve and the address-index reserve a word
// neither uses (see that const's own comment above). `../gc.bit` instead owns
// one dedicated state word, `gcSpanFree`, the same shape #3292 used to grow
// `gcWords` for a single caller-specific field. That is also why these two
// functions take a plain `head: *i64` rather than the reserve descriptor `r`
// every other function here takes — this list is not part of any reserve's
// own bookkeeping, and `reserveTake`/`reserveGrow` never consult it, which is
// what keeps `_tests_/stress/gcreserve`'s "64 span-sized takes are pairwise
// distinct" proof (no freeing in between) unaffected by this file.

// Push a released, `allocSpanBytes`-sized, `allocSpanBytes`-aligned span onto
// the free list rooted at `*head`. See the section header above for why this
// decommits-and-relinks rather than unmapping.
@nosplit fn spanFreeListPush(head: *i64, addr: int) {
  if (bit_rt_remap_pages(i64(addr), i64(allocSpanBytes)) == 0) {
    // The OS refused a MAP_FIXED reset of a range this reserve already owns
    // — should not happen. Fall back to the pre-#3868 behaviour: unmap it and
    // let the address go, rather than link in a span that might still hold
    // the stale mapping.
    memUnmapPages(addr, allocSpanBytes)
    return
  }
  storeWord(addr, *head)
  *head = addr
}

// Pop an address off the free list rooted at `*head`, or 0 when empty. The
// memory is already mapped and zeroed — `spanFreeListPush`'s remap — so the
// caller needs no further action before treating it as a fresh span.
@nosplit fn spanFreeListTake(head: *i64): int {
  let addr = *head
  if (addr == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  *head = loadWord(addr)
  return addr
}

// Unmap every chunk this reserve mapped, and report how many bytes went back to
// the OS. The reserve is EMPTY afterwards, not merely rewound: `resEnd` and
// `resBump` are zeroed, so a later `reserveTake` returns 0 and a later
// `reserveGrow` maps a fresh chunk rather than bumping into freed address
// space.
//
// MEMORY A CALLER SUPPLIED TO `reserveInit` IS NEVER TOUCHED, and that is the
// reason this walks a list instead of unmapping `[base, end)`. The collector's
// first arena belongs to `boot`, and `_tests_/stress/gcindexchurn` binds a
// Bit-managed `[]i64` as its own. Only `reserveGrow` pushes, so neither can
// reach this loop — a distinction the previous "no chunk list" shape could not
// have made at all.
export @nosplit fn reserveRelease(r: *i64): int {
  let freed = 0
  let c = *(r + resChunks)
  // Bounded: one iteration per chunk this reserve mapped, each of which is at
  // least `reserveChunkBytes` of address space the OS handed back.
  while (c != 0) {
    let next = loadWord(c + chunkNextOff)   // read BEFORE the chunk is unmapped
    let nbytes = loadWord(c + chunkLenOff)
    memUnmapPages(c, nbytes)
    freed = freed + nbytes
    c = next
  }
  *(r + resChunks) = 0
  *(r + resEnd) = 0
  *(r + resBump) = 0
  return freed
}

// How many chunks this reserve has mapped from the OS right now, and their
// total mapped bytes (#3046). This is the number #2803's RSS investigation
// spent days without: RSS undercounted a paged-out footprint ~8x, per-class
// occupancy only ever saw OWNED spans (an abandoned chunk holds no live
// objects, so it is invisible to that instrument by construction), and a
// mutation that moved this number 905x (`allocSpanKeep` 2 -> 1000000 took
// chunks 11762 -> 13) moved neither.
//
// A WALK, NOT A NEW FIELD: the three-word descriptor is fixed width
// (`reserveWords` above, and `gc.bit`'s `gcWords == 39` boot guard), and the
// chunk list is already threaded through the chunks themselves, so counting
// it costs nothing but the same walk `reserveRelease` already pays — this is
// that walk with the `munmap` left out, which is also what makes it safe to
// call at any time, including mid-run, unlike `reserveRelease`.
//
// Bounded: one iteration per chunk this reserve has mapped, exactly as
// `reserveRelease`'s loop above.
//
// PINNED (ABI.md §9), same placeholder convention as `reserveInit`/`reserveTake`/
// `reserveGrow` above: `./gccollect.bit`'s `gcSpanChunks` forwards this one
// level (a plain wrapper, not a word load), so it survives being inlined into
// that forwarder and then referenced from `runtime/stw`, a separately emitted
// `--freestanding` object — an unpinned export there is `m<N>$name`, a name no
// sibling object can define (SPEC §11.9). `bit_rt_port_` is not an ABI name,
// so no rename (#1583) collides it with an RtFn target.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_mem_reserve_chunk_count") fn reserveChunkCount(r: *i64): int {
  let n = 0
  let c = *(r + resChunks)
  while (c != 0) {
    n = n + 1
    c = loadWord(c + chunkNextOff)
  }
  return n
}

// The companion walk for total mapped bytes, kept as its own function rather
// than an output parameter so both match every other single-value accessor in
// this file (`reserveRemaining` etc.) — the caller who wants only the count
// does not pay for reading the length word too. PINNED for the same reason as
// `reserveChunkCount` above.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_mem_reserve_chunk_bytes") fn reserveChunkBytesMapped(r: *i64): int {
  let bytes = 0
  let c = *(r + resChunks)
  while (c != 0) {
    bytes = bytes + loadWord(c + chunkLenOff)
    c = loadWord(c + chunkNextOff)
  }
  return bytes
}

// Map `nbytes` bytes straight from the OS, bypassing the reserve. This is the
// direct-mapped (large) allocation path, and it is direct for the same reason
// `runtime/alloc/`'s `allocLarge` is: the block is page-granular and individually
// unmappable, so routing it through a bump reserve would make it permanently
// unreclaimable (#1641).
@nosplit fn memMapPages(nbytes: int): int {
  if (nbytes <= 0) {
    return 0
  }
  return int(bit_rt_map_pages(i64(nbytes)))
}

// Release a block `memMapPages` returned, `runtime/alloc/`'s `freeLarge` half of the
// pair (#1641). `addr`/`nbytes` MUST be exactly what the matching map returned
// and computed — `allocLargeLen` is evaluated identically on both sides, which
// is why it was split out of the accounting in the first place.
//
// The result is discarded: the
// range is one this runtime mapped itself, so the only failures munmap can
// report are impossible here, and there is nowhere in a `@nosplit` body to
// report them to anyway.
@nosplit fn memUnmapPages(addr: int, nbytes: int) {
  if (addr == 0 || nbytes <= 0) {
    return
  }
  let _ = bit_rt_unmap_pages(i64(addr), i64(nbytes))
}

// Terminate the process on heap exhaustion, loudly (#1642). NEVER RETURNS.
//
// Callers still write `return 0` after calling this. That is dead code, and it
// is kept deliberately: Bit's unmanaged subset has no `noreturn`, so the checker
// requires every path to produce the declared result, and a `return 0` that can
// never execute is a better shape than pretending the door is fallible.
@nosplit fn memOutOfMemory() {
  let _ = bit_rt_oom()
}

// Bytes still available. Reported so a test can prove a churning workload is
// bounded by its live set rather than by the reserve slowly draining — which is
// the property that distinguishes real free-list recycling from a leak.
export @nosplit fn reserveRemaining(r: *i64): int {
  let end = *(r + resEnd)
  let bump = *(r + resBump)
  return end - bump
}
